    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "\"Cologne,\" \"perfume,\" and \"eau de toilette\" get used almost interchangeably in everyday conversation, which is exactly why the terms feel confusing. The confusion isn't really about chemistry — it's about the word \"cologne\" carrying two very different meanings depending on context." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Quick Answer: What Each Term Actually Means", id: "quick-answer" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Perfume: Used generically to mean any fragrance, but technically refers to the parfum concentration category (20-30% oil)",
        "Cologne (technical): Eau de Cologne, a specific, older, low-concentration category (2-5% oil), historically fresh and citrus-forward",
        "Cologne (casual/common usage): A general, especially North American, catch-all term for any men's fragrance, regardless of its actual concentration",
        "Eau de Toilette: A defined mid-low concentration (5-15% oil), used across all genders of fragrance marketing",
      ] },
      { type: "callout", variant: "info", title: "Two Different 'Colognes'", text: "When someone casually says 'put on some cologne,' they usually mean any men's fragrance — which might technically be an eau de toilette or eau de parfum. The historical, technical 'Eau de Cologne' category is a specific, much lighter concentration that's less commonly used as a label today." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Where the Confusion Comes From", id: "where-confusion-comes-from" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Eau de Cologne originated in 18th-century Germany as a specific, light, citrus-based fragrance style. Over time, especially in North American English, \"cologne\" drifted from naming that specific style into functioning as a generic term for men's fragrance overall, regardless of concentration — much like \"perfume\" drifted into a generic term for fragrance in general, regardless of category." },
      { type: "productGrid", productSlugs: ["amber-oud-eau-de-parfum"] },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "How the Terms Relate to Concentration", id: "terms-and-concentration" },
      { type: "table", caption: "Term vs. Actual Concentration", headers: ["Term", "Common Usage", "Technical Concentration"], rows: [
        ["Perfume", "Any fragrance, generically", "Parfum: 20-30% oil"],
        ["Cologne", "Any men's fragrance, generically", "Eau de Cologne: 2-5% oil"],
        ["Eau de Toilette", "Specific, defined category", "5-15% oil"],
        ["Eau de Parfum", "Specific, defined category", "15-20% oil"],
      ] },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Does Gendered Marketing Reflect Real Differences?", id: "gendered-marketing" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Marketing conventions often label similar concentrations differently based on target gender (a men's 'cologne' and a women's 'eau de toilette' may be chemically similar in concentration)",
        "There's no chemical rule requiring men's fragrances to use lower concentrations than women's",
        "Fragrance families and marketing aesthetics differ more consistently by gender-targeting than actual concentration does",
        "The most reliable way to know what you're buying is to check the actual concentration printed on the bottle, not the marketing category",
      ] },
      { type: "callout", variant: "tip", title: "Check the Label, Not the Marketing Term", text: "Whatever a bottle is casually marketed as, the printed concentration (Parfum, EDP, EDT, or EDC) tells you what you're actually getting far more reliably than the word used in advertising or conversation." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Using the Terms Correctly Going Forward", id: "using-terms-correctly" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "In casual conversation, using \"cologne\" or \"perfume\" generically isn't wrong — that's simply how common usage has evolved. But when shopping or comparing products, it's more useful to think in terms of the specific concentration categories (parfum, EDP, EDT, EDC) than the gendered, colloquial labels." },
      { type: "prosCons", pros: [
        "Checking the actual printed concentration when comparing fragrances",
        "Understanding that 'cologne' has both a technical and a casual, generic meaning",
        "Recognizing that gendered marketing terms don't always reflect real concentration differences",
      ], cons: [
        "Assuming 'cologne' always means a specific low concentration in everyday conversation",
        "Assuming men's and women's fragrances differ chemically in concentration by default",
        "Relying on marketing terminology alone instead of checking the actual label",
      ] },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The words people use for fragrance in casual conversation and the technical concentration categories are two different systems — knowing both helps you shop smarter." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Final Verdict", id: "final-verdict" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "\"Cologne\" and \"perfume\" are used generically in everyday speech, while eau de toilette and eau de parfum refer to specific, defined concentration categories. When it actually matters — like comparing strength or longevity — check the label's concentration rather than relying on the casual term." },
      { type: "newsletterCta" },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: "Is cologne always weaker than perfume?", answer: "Not necessarily in casual usage — 'cologne' is often used generically for any men's fragrance, which could technically be an EDT, EDP, or even parfum concentration." },
      { question: "What does Eau de Cologne technically mean?", answer: "It's a specific, historically light concentration category (roughly 2-5% fragrance oil), originating from 18th-century Germany, typically fresh and citrus-forward." },
      { question: "Is 'perfume' only for women's fragrance?", answer: "No — 'perfume' is often used generically for any fragrance, though it technically refers to the parfum concentration category, which isn't gender-specific." },
      { question: "Do men's and women's fragrances differ in actual concentration?", answer: "Not by any chemical rule — concentration categories (EDT, EDP, parfum) apply regardless of gendered marketing, though marketing conventions sometimes create the impression of a difference." },
      { question: "How can I know what concentration I'm actually buying?", answer: "Check the label printed on the bottle or box (Parfum, EDP, EDT, or EDC) rather than relying on the casual term used in marketing or conversation." },
      { question: "Why do people use 'cologne' and 'perfume' so loosely?", answer: "Both terms have drifted from their specific technical meanings into generic, everyday usage over time, similar to how many product category names evolve in common speech." },
      { question: "Is it wrong to call any fragrance 'perfume' or 'cologne' casually?", answer: "No — that's simply how common usage has evolved, and it's not incorrect in everyday conversation, even though it doesn't precisely match the technical categories." },
      { question: "Which term should I use when comparing fragrance products?", answer: "The specific concentration category (parfum, EDP, EDT, EDC) is more useful and precise than the casual, generic terms when actually comparing products." },
    ],
