    faqs: [
      { question: "Why does my perfume not last as long as advertised?", answer: "Marketing claims are general averages — your specific skin type, hydration level, and environmental conditions all affect actual longevity, sometimes significantly." },
      { question: "Does dry skin really make fragrance fade faster?", answer: "Yes — oil helps slow the evaporation of fragrance compounds, so drier skin tends to hold scent for a shorter time than oilier skin." },
      { question: "Do woody fragrances last longer than citrus fragrances?", answer: "Generally yes — base-note-heavy fragrances like woods and musks evaporate more slowly than top-note-heavy, citrus-forward fragrances." },
      { question: "Can moisturizing before applying perfume actually extend longevity?", answer: "Yes — hydrated skin gives fragrance more to cling to, which can noticeably extend how long a scent lasts compared to applying on dry skin." },
      { question: "Is it normal for the same perfume to last different amounts of time on different people?", answer: "Yes, this is completely normal and expected, due to differences in individual skin chemistry, oil levels, and hydration." },
      { question: "Does hot weather make perfume last shorter or longer?", answer: "It can go either way — heat often increases projection and evaporation speed, which can sometimes mean the scent fades sooner overall despite smelling stronger initially." },
      { question: "Does applying fragrance to hair make it last longer?", answer: "Hair can hold fragrance differently than skin and sometimes extends perceived longevity, though it's worth checking whether a specific fragrance is suitable for hair application." },
      { question: "Can old or improperly stored perfume lose its longevity?", answer: "Yes — heat, light, and humidity exposure over time can degrade a fragrance's composition, sometimes affecting how it performs and lasts." },
    ],
