    title: "Where to Apply Perfume for the Best Results",
    excerpt: "Not all skin is equal when it comes to fragrance. Here's exactly where to apply perfume — and where to avoid — for the best diffusion and longevity.",
    summary: "Pulse points — wrists, neck, inner elbows, behind the ears, and behind the knees — are the most effective application areas because their warmth helps diffuse fragrance, while a few areas like directly on jewelry or broken skin should be avoided.",
    keyTakeaways: [
      { text: "Pulse points (wrists, neck, inner elbows, behind ears, behind knees) are warmer and diffuse fragrance more effectively than other skin areas." },
      { text: "Hair and clothing can hold fragrance well too, but check fabric compatibility and hair-safe formulations first." },
      { text: "Avoid spraying directly onto jewelry, since alcohol in fragrance can damage certain metals and stones over time." },
      { text: "Avoid applying fragrance to broken, irritated, or freshly shaved skin, which can sting or react." },
      { text: "Combining two or three pulse points, rather than one heavy application, gives more balanced diffusion throughout the day." },
    ],
