    title: "How to Layer Perfumes",
    excerpt: "Layering lets you build a more personal, complex scent from products you already own. Here's how to do it intentionally instead of by accident.",
    summary: "Perfume layering means intentionally combining two fragrances (or a fragrance with matching-scent products) to create a more personal, complex, or longer-lasting result — done well, it's a way to customize scent without needing a niche, one-of-a-kind bottle.",
    keyTakeaways: [
      { text: "Layering means intentionally combining two or more fragrances, or a fragrance with matching-scent body products, to build a customized result." },
      { text: "Start with the heavier, more dominant fragrance first, then add a lighter one on top for the best balance." },
      { text: "Complementary or contrasting fragrance families (like a woody base with a citrus top) often layer more successfully than two very similar scents." },
      { text: "Test any layering combination on skin and wait before deciding — combinations can smell different once they've had time to develop." },
      { text: "Layering with matching-scent lotions or oils is a lower-risk starting point than combining two entirely different fragrances." },
    ],
    heroImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523293182086-7651a899d37f?w=1200&q=80",
