    viewCount: 1620,
    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Projection is one of the most commonly confused fragrance terms, often used as a synonym for sillage. They're related, but projection specifically describes something slightly different: how far a fragrance radiates outward from your body at any given moment, rather than the trail it leaves as you move." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Quick Answer: What Projection Means", id: "quick-answer" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Projection is the size of the scent 'bubble' radiating outward from your body while standing still",
        "It's closely related to, but distinct from, sillage, which focuses more on the trail left behind while moving",
        "High projection means people can smell you from a noticeable distance; low projection means it's only detectable up close",
        "Both projection and sillage generally decrease as a fragrance wears through the day",
      ] },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Projection vs. Sillage", id: "projection-vs-sillage" },
      { type: "callout", variant: "info", title: "A Useful (Imperfect) Distinction", text: "Think of projection as the fragrance's presence around you right now, and sillage as the trail it leaves as you move away. In casual conversation the terms are often used interchangeably, but this distinction is useful for more precisely describing how a fragrance behaves." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What Affects Projection", id: "what-affects-projection" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Concentration: Higher fragrance oil percentages generally project further",
        "Quantity applied: More sprays increase projection more directly than they increase overall longevity",
        "Specific materials: Certain synthetic musks and ambroxan-type materials are specifically known for strong projection",
        "Skin type and body temperature: Warmer skin tends to project fragrance more actively than cooler skin",
      ] },
      { type: "productGrid", productSlugs: ["amber-oud-eau-de-parfum"] },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "How Projection Changes Over Time", id: "projection-over-time" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Projection is typically strongest in the first 30-60 minutes after application, when top and early middle notes are most active, then gradually settles closer to the skin as the fragrance moves into its base notes. A fragrance can still be detectable and long-lasting on skin (good longevity) even after its projection has significantly decreased." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Choosing the Right Projection Level", id: "choosing-projection-level" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Office or shared indoor spaces: Lower projection is more considerate of people around you",
        "Evening events, dates, or outdoor settings: Moderate to higher projection can feel more appropriate and noticeable",
        "If people frequently ask what you're wearing from a few feet away, that's a sign of well-balanced, moderate projection",
        "If people comment on your fragrance from across a room consistently, you may want to scale back application",
      ] },
      { type: "table", caption: "Projection Levels and Contexts", headers: ["Projection Level", "Best Suited For"], rows: [
        ["Low", "Office, close quarters, conservative settings"],
        ["Moderate", "Most everyday situations, versatile default"],
        ["High", "Evenings, outdoor events, cold weather (which mutes projection naturally)"],
      ] },
      { type: "prosCons", pros: [
        "Understanding projection as distinct from, but related to, sillage",
        "Choosing projection level based on your specific environment",
        "Recognizing that projection naturally decreases hours after application",
      ], cons: [
        "Using 'projection' and 'sillage' as if they mean exactly the same thing without any distinction",
        "Applying high-projection fragrance heavily in small, shared indoor spaces",
        "Assuming projection stays constant throughout the day without decreasing",
      ] },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Projection is really about reading the room — the same fragrance can be perfect at a dinner and too much on a crowded train." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Final Verdict", id: "final-verdict" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Projection describes how far a fragrance radiates outward from your body, closely related to but distinct from sillage. Match your projection level to your setting, and remember it naturally decreases as the fragrance moves through its notes over the day." },
      { type: "newsletterCta" },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: "What's the difference between projection and sillage?", answer: "Projection describes how far a fragrance radiates outward from your body at a given moment, while sillage more specifically describes the trail left behind as you move." },
      { question: "How can I increase my fragrance's projection?", answer: "Use a higher concentration, apply a few more sprays, or choose a fragrance built with materials known for strong projection, like certain musks." },
      { question: "Does projection stay the same all day?", answer: "No — projection is typically strongest in the first 30-60 minutes after application and gradually decreases as the fragrance settles closer to the skin." },
      { question: "Is high projection always better?", answer: "Not necessarily — it depends on context, since high projection can feel overwhelming in small, shared indoor spaces like offices or elevators." },
      { question: "Why do some fragrances project more than others at the same concentration?", answer: "Specific fragrance materials, like certain synthetic musks and ambroxan-type compounds, are known for strong projection independent of overall concentration." },
      { question: "Does body temperature affect projection?", answer: "Yes — warmer skin tends to project fragrance more actively than cooler skin, since warmth helps volatilize aromatic compounds." },
      { question: "How do I know if my fragrance's projection is too strong?", answer: "If people consistently comment on your fragrance from across a room, it may be a sign to scale back your application for more moderate projection." },
      { question: "Can a fragrance have good longevity but low projection?", answer: "Yes — a fragrance can remain detectable on your own skin for a long time even after its projection into the surrounding air has significantly decreased." },
    ],
